Imagine reporting abusive content on a social media platform, hoping it will be taken down, only to find that it remains online or the response offers little reassurance.

For women experiencing technology-facilitated violence, digital platforms are not just where abuse happens. Their policies, reporting tools and responses can influence whether survivors feel protected or abandoned.

The Digital Harm Effect report examines accountability among technology companies and the role platforms must play in addressing technology-facilitated violence against women and girls.

Platforms provide spaces for women to connect, learn, advocate and participate in public life. They must also take responsibility for addressing the abuse that occurs within those spaces.

What does digital safety mean if the platforms women rely on fail to respond effectively when abuse occurs?

Responsibility cannot rest on survivors alone. Technology companies must strengthen reporting mechanisms, respond appropriately to abuse and make their safety policies meaningful in practice. Governments and civil society also have roles in demanding transparency and accountability.

Women deserve digital spaces where their participation is protected, their reports are taken seriously and their safety is not treated as an afterthought.

Read the Technology-Facilitated Violence Against Women: Africa Digital Harm Report and explore what the experiences of women in Nigeria and Kenya reveal about the future of digital safety, inclusion and participation.https://t.co/IxqfU3KghW reference: Accountability with Tech Companies, p. 46.
